      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1369",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Pangasius macronema Bleeker, 1850</li><li>Common name: Haiwels (Germany)</li><li>Type locality: Bandjarmasin, Kalimantan, Borneo, Indonesia</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2019)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1369",
      "html": "<p>Asia: Mekong, Peninsular Malaysia, and the Greater Sunda Islands rivers of Java, Sumatra and Borneo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1369",
      "html": "<p>Needs a large amount of open space for swimming room and some plants to help reduce the nervous nature of the fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1369",
      "html": "<p>Feeds on mollusks and chironomid insects.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1369",
      "html": "<p>Females are fuller-bodied.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1369",
      "html": "<p>Unreported. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1369",
      "html": "<p>Identification: relatively deep body; lacks mid-lateral and abdominal stripes; lacks a black spot at the dorsal-fin origin; teeth on the roof of the mouth in 4 separate patches (2 vomerine and 3 palatine patches); the maxillary barbel reaches the tip of the pectoral fin.</p><p>Etymology: the name Pangasius comes from a local (Bengali) name of the fish (pungas).</p>"
    },
    {
